WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:05.320
Star Trek, I Love Lucy, and The Prisoner are
some of the greatest television shows of all

00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:05.820
time.

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:10.740
But they also share another surprising connection:
each of those classic shows has had an episode

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:11.240
banned.

00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:12.220
And they aren't alone.

00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:16.280
So what could cause a beloved series like
Mister Rogers' Neighborhood to to get yanked

00:00:16.290 --> 00:00:17.360
from the airwaves?

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:19.980
Here's a look at why these TV episodes were
banned.

00:00:20.540 --> 00:00:22.320
Star Trek: Patterns of Force

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:26.620
The original Star Trek was ambitious in its
attempts at social commentary.

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:31.020
With the episode Patterns of Force, though,
some folks in Germany thought those efforts

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:32.020
went too far.

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:36.840
That's because the episode involved an alien
planet under Nazi-like rule - so Nazi-like

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:39.900
they even used actual Nazi uniforms and symbols.

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:44.600
Luckily Kirk and Spock intervene and set up
a peaceful government instead, but that didn't

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:47.680
keep this episode from being banned in Germany
for decades.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:50.340
I Love Lucy: The Ricardos Visit Cuba

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:56.340
When Lucy, Ricky, and family traveled to Cuba
in 1956, everybody in America had a big chuckle

00:00:56.340 --> 00:00:58.900
at Lucy's hilarious fish-out-of-water hijinks.

00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:04.100
But after Cuba became a communist regime under
Fidel Castro, people stopped laughing, and

00:01:04.119 --> 00:01:08.580
this episode was banned from reruns for "almost
a decade" despite the fact that it's essentially

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:11.040
just one bad cigar gag after another.

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:13.380
Mr. Rogers' Neighborhood: Conflict

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:18.760
The central focus of Mr. Rogers' Neighborhood
was always on teaching moral lessons to children

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:20.180
through stories and songs.

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:22.400
So how do you explain the Cold War to kids?

00:01:22.580 --> 00:01:27.060
Why, with a multi-part epic storyline where
King Friday begins stockpiling bomb-making

00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:30.940
materials because he believes Southwood is
gearing up to attack Make-Believe.

00:01:31.060 --> 00:01:36.200
The result sounds more like Team America than
Mr. Rogers, with puppets engaging in espionage,

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:40.260
political paranoia, and teaching lessons on
air raids and gas mask use.

00:01:40.580 --> 00:01:46.080
"Last time we pretended the people of Southwood
were very much like the people of the neighborhood

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:46.820
of Make-Believe.

00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:52.160
They certainly weren't people who were building
bombs for a war the way King Friday thought

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:52.660
they were."

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:58.160
The episodes actually continued to air in
normal rotation until 1996, but haven't been

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:02.740
seen since, other than a few recent attempts
to use them as current political commentary.

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:05.660
Star Trek: The Next Generation: The High Ground

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:10.560
This episode of Star Trek: The Next Generation
featured Picard and the gang getting involved

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:14.800
in a civil war that was a very thinly veiled
commentary on the Irish Troubles.

00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:19.300
So thinly veiled, in fact, that the planet's
fictional timeline is actually a timeline

00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:23.060
of the Troubles, and Data even directly references
the conflict.

00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:27.140
"And it appears that terrorism is an effect
way to promote political change.

00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:30.730
Yet there are numerous examples when it was
successful.

00:02:30.730 --> 00:02:36.260
The independents of the Mexican state from
Spain, the Irish unification of 2024."

00:02:36.340 --> 00:02:40.791
That didn't go over well in the UK or the
Republic of Ireland, especially since the

00:02:40.791 --> 00:02:45.890
episode aired just a few months after an IRA
bomb attack at the Royal Marines Music School

00:02:45.890 --> 00:02:49.700
in Deal in southern England that killed 11
and wounded 22.

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:55.060
As a result, the episode was banned for almost
two decades before finally airing in 2006.

00:02:55.070 --> 00:02:59.250
Ironically, the episode was originally meant
to be about the American Revolution.

00:02:59.250 --> 00:03:03.550
In the book Captain's Logs, writer Melinda
Snodgrass explained that she was ordered to

00:03:03.550 --> 00:03:04.980
make the change by her bosses.

00:03:05.140 --> 00:03:09.320
I wanted it with Picard as Cornwallis and
the Romulans would have been the French, who

00:03:09.320 --> 00:03:12.300
were in our revolution, trying to break this
planet away.

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:15.000
Suddenly Picard realized he's one of the oppressors.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:18.940
Instead, we do 'Breakfast in Belfast,' where
our people decide they're going to go off

00:03:18.940 --> 00:03:19.900
to Northern Ireland."

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:22.080
The Prisoner: Living in Harmony

00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:27.240
This episode of the surreal series The Prisoner
featured a novel method of trying to psychologically

00:03:27.250 --> 00:03:31.940
break down the protagonist Number 6 by placing
him within a virtual reality simulation of

00:03:31.940 --> 00:03:35.700
an Old West community and trying to coerce
him into playing the role of sheriff.

00:03:35.780 --> 00:03:39.960
The character refuses, though, which may or
may not have led to the episode being banned,

00:03:40.020 --> 00:03:45.080
as some felt it was a commentary about conscientious
objectors avoiding the Vietnam War draft.

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:49.600
Officially, the episode was actually banned
because of the show's portrayal of mind-altering

00:03:49.610 --> 00:03:54.610
drugs, but since many episodes dealt with
drug use and still aired, sensitivity about

00:03:54.610 --> 00:03:57.320
Vietnam seems to be a more likely explanation.

00:03:57.960 --> 00:03:59.040
The Big Bang Theory

00:03:59.460 --> 00:04:02.480
China is pretty ban-happy when it comes to
imported shows.

00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:06.160
They don't need much of a reason, which is
good, because they didn't have much of a reason

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:09.500
when it comes to outright banning The Big
Bang Theory entirely.

00:04:09.500 --> 00:04:14.300
The show is actually quite popular in China,
or at least, it was until the State Administration

00:04:14.300 --> 00:04:18.740
of Press, Publication, Radio, Film and Television
pulled the plug.

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:24.000
Exactly why is a matter of debate, but some
feel it's because of the rise of diaosi, which

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:28.300
originally was a pejorative term for broke
loser guys without jobs or girlfriends.

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:32.320
Now, though, the term has been embraced by
a generation of young geeks proud of their

00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:36.940
so-called loser status who see the characters
in The Big Bang Theory as role models.

00:04:37.100 --> 00:04:38.500
And that scares China.

00:04:38.580 --> 00:04:43.479
Which is great, because the idea of Sheldon
Cooper as a counter-cultural iconoclast threatening

00:04:43.479 --> 00:04:47.910
to destabilize the social order of one of
the biggest nations on Earth is actually more

00:04:47.910 --> 00:04:50.860
entertaining than any plot the show could
have ever come up with.

00:04:51.780 --> 00:04:52.560
"Bazinga"

